  "edition": "arXiv:2609.06906v1",
  "primary_url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1.p4.3",
  "synthesis": "The canonical-KL portion is a small measure-level adapter, using actual current32 density/entropy domains plus existing TiltedKL/Mathlib APIs. It must prove finite ENNReal klDiv and the real entropy identity together; a toReal identity alone is not finiteness. rhoL1r and the -E_r rho-logZ formula are useful equivalent internal outputs, not an extra source assumption. This unlocks the entropy input shared by future Gaussian function-LSI and Talagrand-T2 consumers; it proves neither inequality or W2.",
  "seven_semantic_slots": {
    "objects": "Actual source R_eta,y=mu.tilt(-norm(x-y)^2/(2eta)); one true proximal p; rho fromEx8; r=((x-p)/sqrteta)#R, gamma=N(0,I), Z=integralexp(-rho)gamma, q=exp(-rho)/Z, f=sqrtq. Canonical InformationTheory.klDiv r gamma and MeasureTheory.llr r gamma are measure objects, not newly selected smooth densities.",
    "domains": "Source C2V, normalized Hessian kappa^-1I..I; finite real Euclidean space, eta>0. Current32 prospectively supplies qlogqL1gamma, f/gradfL2gamma, gradrhoL2r, positiveZ and r=qgamma. Canonical KL requires r<<gamma and llrL1r. Finite-Hilbert/Borel/E0 and measurableS are disclosed authored extensions; fixed eta/y is recovered.",
    "quantifiers": "All positive fixedeta and everyy. Internal p/law/partition/domain production precedes output; pointwise parameter-family application adds no jointposterior-kernel/selector claim. Any generic measurable-density adapter is sufficient authored background, not a printed theorem or a supplied canonicalKL certificate.",
    "assumptions": "Only printed standing C2/curvature/normalization and eta>0 at source anchor. True domains, probability, positivepartition, RN identification and KL finite are internally derived via actual parents. No assumed entropy, KL, canonical-score differentiability, LSI/T2 or Wasserstein output.",
    "conclusion": "Prospective narrow endpoint: klDiv(r,gamma)!=top; (klDiv(r,gamma)).toReal=integral logq dr=integral qlogq dgamma=-integralrho dr-logZ; optional rhoL1r/logqL1r and AE llr=logq. No endpoint is compiled/approved by this preread.",
    "scope": "Printed first4.6 is W2(r,gamma)<=sqrt(integralnormgradrho²dr), obtained by GaussianLSI and Talagrand. Entropy identification is omitted analytic background. It is only one portion of METRIC-KL-ADAPTER-GAP, with actual metric/transport and Sobolev representation obligations still separate.",
    "constant_dependencies": "gamma covarianceI, Q=normu²/2+rho, q normalized by Gaussianrelative Z exactly once. Probability masses remove KL finite-measure +nu(univ)-mu(univ) correction. No extra logZV or affine-Jacobian term survives in relative logq. LSI intended Entgamma(f²)<=2Egamma and Egamma=I/4 imply KL<=I/2; T2 intendedW2²<=2KL then gives coefficient1. Those inequalities remain open."
  },
  "printed_source_vs_authored_detail": [
    {
      "anchors": [
        "S4.Ex8",
        "S4.SS1.p4.2"
      ],
      "printed": "Defines residual and actual standardized law proportional to exp(-norm²/2-rho), with Hessian0..eta.",
      "authored": "Exact Gaussian-relative Z/q/f and finite classical entropy domains provide a literal representative rather than proportional-density prose."
    },
    {
      "anchors": [
        "S4.SS1.p4.3",
        "S4.E6"
      ],
      "printed": "Names Talagrand and Gaussian log-Sobolev to obtain first4.6; does not print a KL integral formula, a canonicalRN representative regularity theorem or a bibliographic theorem number here.",
      "authored": "Finite canonical ENNReal KL plus realentropy equality adapts the genuine current32law to future function-LSI/T2. No fabricated external theorem number."
    }
  ],
  "actual_API_inventory": [
    {
      "id": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.InformationTheory.TiltedKL.finite_klDiv_and_toReal_eq_integral_normalizedLogRatio",
      "actual_contract": "Common SigmaFinite/NeZero base, measurable left exponent, both exp weights L1, and explicit normalized logratio L1lefttilt -> canonicalKL finite and toReal integral.",
      "use": "Base gamma, left=-rho, right=0; righttilt=gamma. All listed domains derive internally from current32, not public source certificates."
    },
    {
      "id": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.InformationTheory.TiltedLogRatio.llr_tilted_tilted_ae",
      "actual_contract": "AE(lefttilt) canonicalllr equals exponent/logpartition difference.",
      "use": "llr(r,gamma)=logq=-rho-logZ r-a.e.; no derivative transport."
    },
    {
      "id": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.InformationTheory.RNLogRatio.toReal_klDiv_eq_integral_logRatio_of_probability",
      "actual_contract": "Probability+AC gives toReal formula without llr-L1.",
      "failure_boundary": "When llr is nonintegrable, canonicalKL=top and both total toReal/top and integral_undef become0. This theorem alone cannot provefiniteKL."
    },
    {
      "id": "InformationTheory.klDiv_ne_top_iff / klDiv_ne_top / toReal_klDiv / toReal_klDiv_of_measure_eq",
      "actual_contract": "Pinned Mathlib requires AC and llrL1 for finite branch; two probabilities cancel mass correction.",
      "use": "Finite branch must be retained explicitly before real arithmetic/T2 use."
    },
    {
      "id": "MeasureTheory.integrable_tilted_iff / integral_tilted / tilted_absolutelyContinuous / absolutelyContinuous_tilted",
      "actual_contract": "Exp-L1 yields weighted-integrability equivalence; tiltAC unconditional, reverseAC with genuine exp-L1.",
      "use": "From qlogqL1gamma, exp(-rho)logq=Z(qlogq) gives logqL1r; weighted integrals then equalentropy."
    },
    {
      "id": "MeasureTheory.integrable_rnDeriv_mul_log_iff / integral_rnDeriv_mul_log / llr_tilted_left / Measure.rnDeriv_tilted_left_self",
      "actual_contract": "AC/SigmaFinite/actualLebesgue decomposition where stated; all identities AE or integral, not pointwise calculus.",
      "use": "Alternative pure RN route, not needed if TiltedKL bridge is reused."
    },
    {
      "id": "AutoSamplingTheory.TechnicalLemmas.InformationTheory.CanonicalRelativeFisher.SmoothFiniteScoreDomain",
      "actual_contract": "Requires differentiability of canonicalllr r-a.e. and squaredcanonicalgradientL1, on EuclideanSpace.",
      "failure_boundary": "Current32 gives smooth logq and classical gradient energy. AE canonicalRN equality does not imply its selected representative has the same classical derivatives; do not synthesize this guarded certificate from AE equality."
    }
  ],
  "route_at_most_seven_steps": [
    "1. Keep true r=gamma.tilt(-rho)=gamma.withDensity(ofRealq) from current32. Reconstruct actual exp-L1 internally (positiveZ rules out integral_undef, or existing nonnegative-residual domination); gamma finite probability gives SigmaFinite/NeZero, and tiltAC gives r<<gamma.",
    "2. Use qlogqL1gamma and exp(-rho)logq=Z*qlogq to produce explicit logqL1r by integrable_tilted_iff. Constant integrability plus logq=-rho-logZ also yields rhoL1r if desired. No newmoment/certificate binder.",
    "3. Apply existing TiltedKL with basegamma,left=-rho,right0. Constant exp0L1, measurableleft and logratio-L1 are genuine internal facts; right zero-tilt is gamma. Obtain KL!=top together with toReal=integrallogqdr.",
    "4. Rewrite integral via actual tilt/density: integrallogqdr=integralqlogqdgamma. Derive -integralrhodr-logZ only after rhoL1r; probability mass1 fixes the constant and cancels finite-measure correction.",
    "5. Use AE llr=logq only for integrability/integral/RN measure identification. Preserve literal C2logq calculus separately; do not infer canonical classical gradients. A future honest function-LSI route can use the explicit smooth sqrtq without canonicalFisher.",
    "6. Future actual GaussianLSI producer plus required genuine function/Sobolev domain adaptation would consume the identified entropy and current32Egamma=I/4, yielding KL<=I/2. Future actualGaussianT2 consumes same canonicalKL and real Euclidean law/metric adapters.",
    "7. Only after both genuine producers and finitecost/metric/sqrt adapters may one derive first4.6 W2<=sqrtI, then use true Ex9/Ex7 mean and coupling domains for prospective bias4.2. Neither result is provided here."
  ],
